# Cross-machine safety rules. This project is developed on two computers (a laptop and a
# Windows PC), so the same source has to produce the same bytes on both, and the generated
# artifacts must never be merged by hand.

# Normalise line endings everywhere. Without this, a Windows checkout with the default
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites index.html's HTML wrapper to CRLF, the build splices in an
# LF bundle, and the two machines emit files that differ by one byte per header line for
# no reason. Checked: the deployed artifact and the dev build differed by exactly 16 bytes
# this way, all of it newlines.
* text=auto eol=lf

# index.html is a ~620KB generated artifact: tools/build.mjs bundles JpnFlashcards.jsx and
# splices it into the inline <script>. It is committed (Netlify/Workers run no build step)
# but it is NOT source.
#
# -merge is the important one. A three-way textual merge of minified JS produces a file
# that still parses and still loads, and then renders a blank or broken page — the exact
# failure tools/build.mjs was written to prevent. With -merge, git leaves the file alone
# and reports a conflict, and the only correct resolution is to rebuild:
#
#     git checkout --ours index.html && cd tools && node build.mjs
#
# Resolve conflicts in JpnFlashcards.jsx, never in index.html.
index.html  -merge -diff linguist-generated

# Same reasoning: a generated data file (tools/yt-index.mjs), meaningless to merge line by
# line. Regenerate it rather than reconciling it — see tools/REFRESH-VIDEO-INDEX.md.
data/videos.json  -merge -diff linguist-generated
